WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.533
Longitudinal-wave questions become much easier once the learner stops picturing the air as a block that travels from source to listener.

2
00:00:06.533 --> 00:00:13.067
In a sound wave, the pressure pattern moves onward while each air particle only oscillates about its own position.

3
00:00:13.067 --> 00:00:19.600
That is why compressions and rarefactions can travel through a tube even though one marked particle only shuffles back and forth.

4
00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:26.133
The key lesson move is to track the pattern and the particle separately before calculating speed or naming the wave type.

5
00:00:26.133 --> 00:00:32.667
Keep this takeaway in view: Lesson 2 gets safer as soon as pattern travel and particle motion are kept separate.

6
00:00:32.667 --> 00:00:39.200
Pattern means compression travel.

7
00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:45.733
The crowded and spread-out regions move through the medium as the wave propagates.

8
00:00:45.733 --> 00:00:52.267
Particle means local oscillation.

9
00:00:52.267 --> 00:00:58.800
One marked air particle only moves back and forth about an equilibrium position.

10
00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:05.461
This diagram matters because it holds the whole longitudinal story in one place.

11
00:01:05.461 --> 00:01:12.122
A compression is a crowded region.

12
00:01:12.122 --> 00:01:18.784
A rarefaction is a spread-out region.

13
00:01:18.784 --> 00:01:25.445
Those regions move through the medium even though one highlighted particle only shuffles back and forth around one position.

14
00:01:25.445 --> 00:01:32.106
Once the learner sees that the particle motion is parallel to the direction of propagation, the wave can be named as longitudinal with much more confidence.

15
00:01:32.106 --> 00:01:38.768
Keep this takeaway in view: A good longitudinal explanation names the pressure pattern and the particle motion separately.

16
00:01:38.768 --> 00:01:45.429
Compression: A crowded pressure region moves through the medium as part of the wave pattern.

17
00:01:45.429 --> 00:01:52.090
Rarefaction: A spread-out region follows and completes the longitudinal pressure cycle.

18
00:01:52.090 --> 00:01:58.045
A queue analogy helps because it separates a moving squeeze from local motion.

19
00:01:58.045 --> 00:02:04.000
If one person pushes, a crowded patch can travel down the line even though each person only shifts a small distance.

20
00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:09.955
Sound in air works in a similar way.

21
00:02:09.955 --> 00:02:15.910
The compression-rarefaction pattern moves onward, but the particles do not travel the whole distance with it.

22
00:02:15.910 --> 00:02:21.865
That is the key reason why saying 'the air moves across the room' is too weak to describe sound propagation accurately.

23
00:02:21.865 --> 00:02:27.820
Keep this takeaway in view: The queue picture protects the lesson from the bulk-transport mistake.

24
00:02:27.820 --> 00:02:33.775
Traveling squeeze means pattern story.

25
00:02:33.775 --> 00:02:39.730
A crowded patch can move down the queue without each person walking the full length.

26
00:02:39.730 --> 00:02:45.685
Local shuffle means particle story.

27
00:02:45.685 --> 00:02:51.640
Each person only steps a little around one place, just like an air particle in the sound wave.

28
00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:58.344
The speed relation for the wave pattern is distance divided by time.

29
00:02:58.344 --> 00:03:05.047
If a compression pattern moves one point two metres in four milliseconds, the wave speed belongs to that moving pattern.

30
00:03:05.047 --> 00:03:11.751
The wave type is decided by comparing particle motion with propagation direction.

31
00:03:11.751 --> 00:03:18.455
When the particle oscillation is parallel to the direction of travel, the sound wave is longitudinal.

32
00:03:18.455 --> 00:03:25.158
Those two ideas work together: one names the geometry, and one measures the motion of the pressure pattern.

33
00:03:25.158 --> 00:03:31.862
Keep this takeaway in view: The formula scene works when the learner can say what is moving fast and what is only oscillating locally.

34
00:03:31.862 --> 00:03:38.565
Wave naming: Use the direction comparison before saying the wave is longitudinal.

35
00:03:38.565 --> 00:03:45.269
Speed reading: Use the distance and time of the moving compression pattern to find the wave speed.

36
00:03:45.269 --> 00:03:51.973
wave speed = pattern distance / time.

37
00:03:51.973 --> 00:03:58.676
longitudinal means particle motion is parallel to propagation.

38
00:03:58.676 --> 00:04:05.380
Give the speed to the moving pattern, not to one particle crossing the whole tube.

39
00:04:05.380 --> 00:04:11.422
A strong routine for this lesson has three steps.

40
00:04:11.422 --> 00:04:17.464
First highlight one particle and say how it moves.

41
00:04:17.464 --> 00:04:23.506
Second highlight one compression or rarefaction band and say how that pattern moves.

42
00:04:23.506 --> 00:04:29.548
Third compare the two directions to classify the wave and then calculate the pattern speed if needed.

43
00:04:29.548 --> 00:04:35.590
This routine keeps the logic orderly and prevents the common collapse where particle displacement, propagation direction, and speed all get blended together.

44
00:04:35.590 --> 00:04:41.632
Keep this takeaway in view: Particle first, pattern second, comparison third is the safest reading routine for Lesson 2.

45
00:04:41.632 --> 00:04:47.674
Step 1 means track one particle.

46
00:04:47.674 --> 00:04:53.716
Describe the local oscillation of one marked particle around its own equilibrium position.

47
00:04:53.716 --> 00:04:59.758
Step 2 means track one band.

48
00:04:59.758 --> 00:05:05.800
Describe how the compression-rarefaction pattern travels through the medium.

49
00:05:05.800 --> 00:05:10.725
Run four checks.

50
00:05:10.725 --> 00:05:15.651
Sound in air is longitudinal.

51
00:05:15.651 --> 00:05:20.576
A compression is a crowded pressure region and a rarefaction is a spread-out region.

52
00:05:20.576 --> 00:05:25.502
The pressure pattern can move along the tube while one particle only oscillates locally.

53
00:05:25.502 --> 00:05:30.427
And wave speed belongs to the moving pattern, not to a single particle traveling across the whole medium.

54
00:05:30.427 --> 00:05:35.353
Those four checks capture the lesson structure before the calculation starts.

55
00:05:35.353 --> 00:05:40.278
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready once the learner can explain what travels and what stays local.

56
00:05:40.278 --> 00:05:45.204
Type means longitudinal.

57
00:05:45.204 --> 00:05:50.129
Particle motion is parallel to the direction of propagation.

58
00:05:50.129 --> 00:05:55.055
Pattern means compression and rarefaction.

59
00:05:55.055 --> 00:05:59.980
These pressure regions move onward as the sound wave propagates.

60
00:05:59.980 --> 00:06:06.233
Start by reading the geometry.

61
00:06:06.233 --> 00:06:12.487
Ask whether the particle motion is parallel or perpendicular to the wave travel.

62
00:06:12.487 --> 00:06:18.740
For sound in air, it is parallel, so the wave is longitudinal.

63
00:06:18.740 --> 00:06:24.993
Then use the distance and time of the moving compression pattern to calculate the wave speed.

64
00:06:24.993 --> 00:06:31.247
Finish by stating clearly that the pattern travels along the tube while the marked particle only oscillates locally.

65
00:06:31.247 --> 00:06:37.500
That sequence is the cleanest route into the worked example.

66
00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:43.753
Keep this takeaway in view: Lesson 2 becomes much easier once the learner can separate local particle motion from wave propagation every time.

67
00:06:43.753 --> 00:06:50.007
Do not skip the geometry: The longitudinal label should come from the direction comparison, not from memory alone.

68
00:06:50.007 --> 00:06:56.260
Do not skip the meaning: Say explicitly that the pressure pattern moves onward while the particle oscillates locally.

69
00:06:56.260 --> 00:07:02.513
compare particle motion with propagation direction.

70
00:07:02.513 --> 00:07:08.767
calculate speed from the moving pattern data.

71
00:07:08.767 --> 00:07:15.020
A strong answer gives the wave type, the speed, and the physical meaning in separate steps.
